fcc59b5: plan §2.2's "Follow descendants of a selected organism".

A click's selection follows the organism's line of descent. While tracking (T), when the organism dies, the selection, camera and inspection pass to its heir: the descendant then alive fewest generations below it, then the eldest. The engine's ancestry index holds the line (World::follow/followed). It is observer state: no digest, checkpoint or report holds it. The session (SessionHandle::follow), the render epochs (Epoch::followed) and the Studio host carry it through. The binary prints `following` and `handed` records.

Evidence (headless; no window opened):
- engine unit tests against a never-pruning genealogy (>30 hand-offs, >10 ended over 4 × 3,000 ticks);
- an Appendix A world to 1,200 ticks, its digests equal to an untraced world's;
- tests/follow.rs: a session's epoch carries exactly the directly stepped world's line after a removal brush takes the parent;
- tests/offscreen.rs: headless Metal. Untracked, nothing is handed on. Tracked, exactly one frame hands the selection to exactly the direct world's heir, centred within 1e-6 px. Its timeline Select is recorded and its inspection answered. The Studio closes on the direct world's digest.
- Planted bugs: 24 of 25 caught. The binary printing no `handed` record survives, because a hand-off needs a click, which only a window gives the binary.
- Gates: fmt, rustdoc, check, clippy -D warnings, and the engine/runtime/render/manim/studio suites, 0 failed.

